WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.640
So we're starting to see sort of a new divide
in the fight against coronavirus.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:10.960
Earlier you had these metro areas with a larger
population densities, more flights directly

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:11.960
to China.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:15.210
You started seeing those areas have more cases
pretty early.

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:18.300
So New York City certainly at the top of that
list.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.930
Boston would be another example as well.

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:25.609
And you've got places that are less connected
to the global economy, having fewer cases

00:00:25.609 --> 00:00:26.609
early on.

00:00:26.609 --> 00:00:31.270
We are now starting to see a divergence where
the numbers are starting to improve pretty

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:35.950
dramatically in some of these early hotspots
in terms of a reduction in cases and a reduction

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:41.030
in deaths and a reduction in serious cases
in people in ICU use.

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:45.440
But we have a new wave of cases in places
that have been insisting all along, hey, I

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:46.580
don't know that we need to close.

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:48.150
We don't have any cases.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:53.720
And very quickly, they are getting many, many
more cases and they still seem to not understand

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:55.460
how viral growth works.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:57.490
Now, let's look at one example, and that's

00:00:57.490 --> 00:01:02.540
Mississippi Republican Governor Tate Reeves
was insisting for weeks.

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:04.730
Mississippi can open we can open Mississippi.

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:06.030
Everything's great here.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:08.870
And indeed, he decided to go ahead and open
businesses.

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:12.750
The cases spiked and he is canceling that
plan.

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:13.810
He's calling it a pause.

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:14.810
But OK.

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:16.370
I mean, eventually businesses will open.

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:18.690
The plan to open now has been canceled.

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:24.830
Friday, Mississippi had about 400 new cases
and 20 new deaths and they have changed their

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:25.830
plans.

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:28.220
Governor Tate Reeves should actually be applauded
for doing this.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:31.659
We shouldn't make fun of the fact that his
initial plan was wrong.

00:01:31.659 --> 00:01:35.700
He should be praised because he's actually
looking at the data and changing the plan.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:40.660
Because when you look at other places that
have started to open and it's still in some

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:42.650
cases, we don't even see the full effect yet.

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:45.930
Places like Florida are quickly finding more
and more new

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:49.590
cases despite still having pretty low levels
of testing.

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:53.640
Virginia is now finding close to a thousand
new cases a day.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:57.240
Texas is finding close to 1000 new cases a
day.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:03.290
And they're only testing a third or a quarter
of what places like Massachusetts and Rhode

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:05.760
Island and New York are testing.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:12.200
If you're only testing 14000 people a day
and you're finding 1000 new cases a day, those

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:16.060
numbers are going to go up even faster as
you start to test more.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:19.959
Indiana is another place that supposedly had
no problem.

00:02:19.959 --> 00:02:21.480
They were all set to go.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:27.530
One of the top growth rates by percentage
now over the weekend, Ohio becoming a hotspot.

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:32.820
Georgia may be the hot spot by the end of
the week as Governor Kemp there has just blown

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:34.170
the doors wide open.

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:37.590
Georgia has nearly 30000 cases now.

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:38.800
I believe that if we had

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:46.030
stayed as close to fully shut down as possible,
just two more weeks everywhere, we would have

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:52.430
been in a good position to have more testing
by then and to be able to start easing restrictions

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:53.900
and guidelines.

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:57.490
But now a lot of these states are going to
fall very, very far.

00:02:57.490 --> 00:03:02.430
Back here in Massachusetts, Republican Governor
Charlie Baker extended the stay at home order.

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:04.630
It was supposed to end today, May 4.

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:08.130
Charlie Baker has extended it by two weeks
to May 18th.

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:10.020
And we've seen cases level off.

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:15.410
We've got about a three percent new case rate
per day and it hasn't been climbing.

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:20.130
We've seen a net decrease in hospitalizations
over the last week.

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:24.860
Deaths have dropped from close to 200 a day
to about 100 a day.

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:25.860
Still a lot.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:28.760
Still tragic, but that's the direction you
want to see things going.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:34.330
My instinct is that in another 14 days, if
Massachusetts keeps doing this, the numbers

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:36.050
are going to look dramatically better.

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:39.270
The opposite seems to be happening in many
southern states.

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:41.750
Now, I want to praise Mississippi.

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:43.600
They had a plan.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:45.410
We thought it was the wrong plan.

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:46.410
Many of us did.

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:47.410
They looked at

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:49.629
the data and they changed the plan.

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:53.920
We can focus on it was a bad plan to start
with and it was a bad plan to start with.

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:57.550
But lots of states have bad plans and many
of them aren't changing the plans.

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:01.580
As the data changes, Georgia, Texas and Florida
are examples.

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:07.680
At least Governor Tate Reeves realized the
plan doesn't match the data and we have to

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:08.680
change our plan.

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:14.849
Mississippi is a place with the worst health
care in the country, according to U.S. News,

00:04:14.849 --> 00:04:16.790
50th out of 50.

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:23.340
They can't risk having their system overwhelmed,
which would lead to a very high death rate.

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:25.889
He changed his mind and that's great.

00:04:25.889 --> 00:04:28.740
Let's see what happens in these other places
as cases spike.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:32.309
Texas and Georgia, we will be following closely
all week.

00:04:32.309 --> 00:04:37.629
Many states now also have armed protesters
demanding that they be reopened.

00:04:37.629 --> 00:04:38.629
Protesters showing

00:04:38.629 --> 00:05:01.869
up, yelling at cops, typically not wearing
facemasks.

00:05:01.869 --> 00:05:56.849
Very, very ugly stuff.

